No open health events, no firing alarms. Your cluster eks (v1.37, Auto Mode, us-east-1) is clean. Here is the complete EKS debugging sequence — structured from first symptom to root cause, covering every layer of the stack:

EKS Must-Have Debugging Command Sequence
The golden rule — always debug in this order
1. Cluster health      →  is the control plane OK?
2. Node health         →  are nodes Ready?
3. Pod health          →  are pods Running?
4. Application health  →  is the app responding?
5. Network health      →  can pods talk to each other?
6. IAM / RBAC          →  are permissions correct?

Never skip layers. A pod issue is often a node issue in disguise. A node issue is often an IAM issue in disguise.

Level 0 — Cluster health
# Is the control plane healthy?
aws eks describe-cluster --name eks --region us-east-1 \
  --query 'cluster.{Status:status,Version:version,Endpoint:endpoint,Logging:logging}'

# Are all addons healthy?
aws eks list-addons --cluster-name eks --region us-east-1
aws eks describe-addon --cluster-name eks --addon-name coredns \
  --query 'addon.{Status:status,Issues:health.issues}'

# Any open AWS health events for EKS?
aws health describe-events \
  --filter services=EKS,regions=us-east-1,eventStatusCodes=open \
  --region us-east-1

# Check control plane logs in CloudWatch
aws logs describe-log-groups \
  --log-group-name-prefix /aws/eks/eks/cluster \
  --region us-east-1

Run in CloudShell
Level 1 — Node debugging
# ── STEP 1: Are nodes Ready? ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
kubectl get nodes -o wide
# Look for: STATUS=Ready, correct VERSION, correct ROLES

# ── STEP 2: Why is a node NotReady? ──────────────────────────────────────────
kubectl describe node <node-name>
# Look for in Events section:
#   NodeNotReady        → kubelet stopped posting status
#   DiskPressure        → node disk > 85% full
#   MemoryPressure      → node memory exhausted
#   PIDPressure         → too many processes
#   NetworkUnavailable  → CNI not initialized (vpc-cni issue)

# ── STEP 3: Node conditions at a glance ──────────────────────────────────────
kubectl get nodes -o json | jq '.items[] | {
  name: .metadata.name,
  conditions: [.status.conditions[] | select(.status=="True") | .type]
}'

# ── STEP 4: What is allocated vs available on each node? ─────────────────────
kubectl describe nodes | grep -A 8 "Allocated resources"

# ── STEP 5: Karpenter NodeClaims (Auto Mode) ─────────────────────────────────
kubectl get nodeclaims
kubectl describe nodeclaim <nodeclaim-name>
# Look for: Status.Conditions — why was this node provisioned/deprovisioned

# ── STEP 6: Karpenter logs — provisioning decisions ──────────────────────────
kubectl logs -n kube-system \
  -l app.kubernetes.io/name=karpenter \
  --since=10m | grep -E "ERROR|WARN|provisioned|disrupted|failed|nodeclaim"

# ── STEP 7: Node resource usage ──────────────────────────────────────────────
kubectl top nodes
# If this fails: metrics-server is not installed

Run in CloudShell
Level 2 — Pod debugging (most common layer)
# ── STEP 1: Find all non-Running pods ────────────────────────────────────────
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v -E "Running|Completed"
# States to investigate:
#   Pending           → scheduler cannot place pod (no node, taints, affinity)
#   ContainerCreating → image pull or CNI issue
#   CrashLoopBackOff  → app is crashing on startup
#   OOMKilled         → memory limit too low
#   Evicted           → node ran out of resources
#   ImagePullBackOff  → cannot pull image (ECR auth, wrong tag)
#   Terminating       → stuck finalizer or node gone

# ── STEP 2: Why is a pod Pending? ────────────────────────────────────────────
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
# Look for in Events section:
#   0/1 nodes available: insufficient cpu     → requests too high
#   0/1 nodes available: node(s) had taint    → taint/toleration mismatch
#   0/1 nodes available: didn't match affinity → nodeSelector/affinity wrong
#   no nodes available                         → Karpenter not provisioning

# ── STEP 3: Why is a pod CrashLoopBackOff? ───────────────────────────────────
# Current logs
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

# Previous crashed container logs (most useful for CrashLoopBackOff)
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --previous

# Tail live logs
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> -f --tail=100

# Multi-container pod — specify container
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> -c <container-name>

# ── STEP 4: Why is a pod OOMKilled? ──────────────────────────────────────────
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> | grep -A 5 "Last State"
# Look for:
#   Reason: OOMKilled   → increase memory limit
#   Exit Code: 137      → OOMKilled confirmation

# ── STEP 5: Pod resource usage ───────────────────────────────────────────────
kubectl top pods -A --containers
# Shows per-container CPU and memory usage
# Compare against requests/limits in the deployment

# ── STEP 6: Pod resource requests vs limits ───────────────────────────────────
kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq -r '
  .items[] |
  .metadata.namespace + "/" + .metadata.name + " → " +
  (.spec.containers[].resources | tostring)'

# ── STEP 7: Pods with NO resource requests (dangerous) ───────────────────────
kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq -r '
  .items[] |
  select(.spec.containers[].resources.requests == null) |
  .metadata.namespace + "/" + .metadata.name + " has NO requests"'

# ── STEP 8: Shell into a running pod ─────────────────────────────────────────
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n <namespace> -- /bin/sh
# If /bin/sh not available:
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n <namespace> -- /bin/bash

# ── STEP 9: Run a debug container on a node ──────────────────────────────────
kubectl debug node/<node-name> -it --image=public.ecr.aws/amazonlinux/amazonlinux:2

